Layer-by-Layer Responsibility
1. controller/ — HTTP Layer
Exposes REST endpoints (e.g., POST /api/subscriptions, GET /api/subscriptions/{id})
Receives HTTP requests, validates input, and returns HTTP responses
Should not contain business logic — just routing and request/response mapping
2. service/ — Business Logic Layer
Implements core subscription rules, e.g.:
Subscription plan activation/cancellation
Billing cycle calculation (monthly/annual)
Integration with the Payment Service (charging customers)
Subscription state transitions (ACTIVE → SUSPENDED → CANCELLED)
3. repository/ — Data Access Layer
Uses Spring Data JPA (likely)
Handles database CRUD operations for subscriptions
Abstracts the underlying database (PostgreSQL, MySQL, etc.)
4. model/ — Domain Layer
Contains JPA entities like Subscription, Plan, User
Likely has an enum for subscription status and DTOs for API requests/responses
Important fields: planType, startDate, endDate, status, price
5. SubscriptionApplication.java — Entry Point
Annotated with @SpringBootApplication
Contains main() method that boots the Spring context
6. pom.xml — Maven Build File
Defines dependencies (spring-boot-starter-web, spring-boot-starter-data-jpa, etc.)
Build/package configuration for producing a runnable JAR
7. Dockerfile — Containerization
Multi-stage build pattern (typical):
Stage 1: Use maven image to compile + package the JAR
Stage 2: Use a slim eclipse-temurin (OpenJDK) image to run the JAR
